---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import PageHero from "../../components/PageHero.astro";
import TopicVolumeMap from "../../components/TopicVolumeMap.astro";
import AboutVolumeAtlas from "../../components/AboutVolumeAtlas.astro";
import { sitePath } from "../../lib/paths";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About"
  description="Mission and editorial principles of Independent Observer."
  currentPath="/about/"
>
  <PageHero
    eyebrow="About the project"
    title="Independence is a method, not a pose."
    description="When public life rewards reflex, spectacle, and retaliation, independent observation creates a disciplined pause: document the record before joining the reaction. Volume I asks how time, resources, institutional access, and economic room shape what becomes visible, then carries that method through power, work, technology, and human capability."
    className="about-hero"
  />
  <section class="section section-navy" aria-labelledby="volume-one-method-title">
    <div class="shell page-grid">
      <aside class="page-aside">Volume I · Method</aside>
      <div class="prose">
        <h2 id="volume-one-method-title">Observe before you react.</h2>
        <p>
          Volume I treats observation as active work, not passive distance. It asks who has the
          time, resources, institutional access, and economic room to watch before reacting—and how
          those conditions shape what becomes visible.
        </p>
        <p>
          The point is not to pretend that a person or archive has no perspective. The point is to
          leave the record open to inspection: define the terms, date the evidence, separate
          documented fact from interpretation, and show what would change the argument.
        </p>
        <h3>Documentation is a public technology.</h3>
        <p>
          A documented record can outlast the immediate controversy. It lets later readers compare
          what was said, what was observed, what was inferred, and what was corrected. That is one
          way history becomes more than repetition.
        </p>
        <p>
          A documented Harvard example is Charles Hamilton Houston: Harvard Law School records
          identify him as the first Black editor of the Harvard Law Review, while archival accounts
          describe his later surveying of conditions and use of film in legal work. The example is
          here for its discipline of building a record before argument—not as proof that any
          observer is free from values or bias. Read the
          <a
            href="https://hls.harvard.edu/today/the-man-who-killed-jim-crow-the-legacy-of-charles-hamilton-houston/"
            target="_blank"
            rel="noopener noreferrer">Harvard Law School account</a
          > and the
          <a
            href="https://www.law.cornell.edu/houston/housbio.htm"
            target="_blank"
            rel="noopener noreferrer">Cornell archive</a
          >.
        </p>
        <div class="about-method-callout">
          <strong>Volume I rule:</strong>
          keep documented fact, interpretation, hypothesis, policy proposal, limitation, counterargument,
          and unresolved question distinguishable.
        </div>
        <p>
          <a href={sitePath("/library/documents/independent-observer-volume-one-ssrn/")}
            >Read the Volume I public copy →</a
          >
          <span aria-hidden="true"> · </span>
          <a href={sitePath("/series/independent-observer/")}>See its place in the series →</a>
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="section section-white">
    <div class="shell page-grid">
      <aside class="page-aside">Mission</aside>
      <div class="prose">
        <h2>Look past the approved frame.</h2>
        <p>
          Public controversies are often presented as isolated disputes between personalities or
          parties. This project instead asks how rules, incentives, historical memory, technology,
          and institutional structure shape the available choices.
        </p>
        <p>
          Independent Observer is a connected four-volume inquiry. Each volume carries the same
          method into a different part of public life, so the project can examine not only what
          happened, but the conditions that made it visible, durable, or difficult to change.
        </p>
        <p>
          Read the four volumes as a sequence: Volume I establishes how to observe; Volume II asks
          where authority and sovereignty are organized; Volume III follows who carries the cost of
          work and social change; Volume IV asks what kind of human capability technology can
          support and who gets to govern it.
        </p>
        <p>
          “Independent” does not mean free from values, error, or perspective. It means the work
          should not conceal its reasoning behind tribal loyalty. Claims should be sourced,
          interpretations should be identifiable, and uncertainty should remain visible.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
  <AboutVolumeAtlas />
  <TopicVolumeMap />
</BaseLayout>
